IT Equipment Checkout System

A small internal tool for tracking IT equipment: who has what, when it's due back, and who needs a reminder. I built it as a portfolio project to practice SQL Server database design, with a thin Flask app on top so the data layer can be seen working end to end.

Stack: SQL Server (Docker) · Python 3 · Flask · pyodbc

What it does
Inventory: items and categories, with a live status (Available / Checked Out).
Check out / check in: each checkout has a due date and a SysAid ticket number. Double checkouts of the same item are blocked in the database, not in the app.
Dashboard: summary tiles, an overdue list sorted by days late, and a list of equipment still held by disabled users.
Email reminders: a script sends Early, Due and Late reminders and logs every attempt.
User import: active users are loaded from an Active Directory-style CSV export through a staging table and a validated merge procedure. AD is simulated with this CSV in the project.
What this demonstrates

Schema design and constraints

Surrogate primary keys, foreign keys, UNIQUE constraints (for example on category names and AD object GUIDs) and DEFAULT values.
Table-level, named CHECK constraints for rules that span columns (such as due date versus checkout date).
Soft delete (IsActive) instead of deleting rows, so history stays intact.
A filtered unique index on ENDUSER.OBJECTGUID, which lets existing rows hold NULL until they are matched.

Stored procedures and transactions

sp_CheckoutItem and sp_CheckinItem change the item's status and the checkout history together, inside one transaction.
Collision handling: the status update only succeeds if the item is still Available, and @@ROWCOUNT is checked, so two people cannot check out the same item.
SET XACT_ABORT ON, TRY/CATCH, XACT_STATE() and THROW, so errors roll back cleanly and reach the caller.
sp_ImportADUsers: validates a staging table first and aborts the whole import if anything is wrong, then runs update, insert and disable steps in a single transaction and records the counts in import_log.
Quick start

Prerequisites: Docker, Python 3, and the Microsoft ODBC Driver 18 for SQL Server.

Start SQL Server (example; use your own password):
   docker run --platform linux/amd64 -e ACCEPT_EULA=Y \
     -e MSSQL_SA_PASSWORD='<your-password>' -e TZ=America/New_York \
     -p 1433:1433 --name sqlserver -d mcr.microsoft.com/mssql/server:2022-latest

--platform linux/amd64 is only needed on Apple Silicon. TZ matters because due dates use the server's local date. 2. Create the database ITCheckoutSystem and run the SQL scripts against it: tables, stored procedures, then views. 3. Install dependencies:

   python3 -m venv venv && source venv/bin/activate
   pip install -r requirements.txt
Configure: copy .env.example to .env and fill in the database and SMTP values.
Run the app:
   python3 app.py

Open http://localhost:5001. (Port 5001 is used because macOS AirPlay occupies 5000.)

Import users from a CSV export: put the file in the Imports/ folder (or set IMPORT_DIR in .env), then:

python3 import_ad_users.py ad_export.csv

Expected columns: ObjectGUID, SamAccountName, DisplayName, Mail, Enabled.

Send reminders:

python3 send_reminders.py

Sending needs real SMTP credentials in .env. Without them, each attempt is logged as failed in Reminder_Log and the script carries on.

Design decisions
History table plus stored status, no trigger. The Checkouts table is the history. ITEMS.Status is a stored copy that only the stored procedures change, which keeps the logic in one visible place.
Soft delete everywhere. Users and items are deactivated, never deleted, so old checkouts always point at a real row.
Reminder policy: 3 days before the due date, on the due date, then every 3 days while late. The query is a CTE, and Reminder_Log stops the same reminder going out twice.
Staging before merge. The staging table has no constraints and accepts any text, so a bad file never fails halfway through loading. Validation queries then find the problems and report them.
Match on ObjectGUID, not username. A username can change, so it is just an attribute. A one-time backfill links existing users to their GUID by username.
Usernames are never reused. Disabled users keep their username. If one were reused, the unique constraint would fail the insert and the transaction would roll back.
Dates use the server's local time. The container runs with TZ=America/New_York, so "today" in SQL matches the user's today.
Reservations are out of scope for this version.
Roadmap and known limitations
No login or authentication. Anyone who can reach the app can use it.
Scheduling: the reminder script and the user import run manually. Next step is cron (or Task Scheduler on Windows).
Reservations (booking an item for a future date) are not supported.
